            <ArticleTitle>Pediatric limb differences and amputations.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>95-108</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmr.2014.09.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1047-9651(14)00103-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Congenital limb differences are uncommon birth defects that may go undetected even with prenatal screening ultrasound scans and often go undetected until birth. For children with congenital limb differences, a diagnostic evaluation should be done to rule out syndromes involving other organ systems or known associations. The most common etiology of acquired amputation is trauma. Postamputation complications include pain and terminal bony overgrowth. A multidisciplinary approach to management with the child and family can lead to a successful, functional, and fulfilling life.</AbstractText>
